<BaseLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/sharia-divorce-uae/"
  {hreflang}
  lang="en"
  schema={articleSchema}
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-4">
    <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />
  </div>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-tag">Islamic Divorce Guide, UAE 2026</p>
      <h1>Sharia Divorce in UAE 2026: Talaq, Khula', Financial Rights, and the New Law</h1>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant &middot; Updated October 2026</p>
      <p class="hero-desc">
        Sharia divorce in the UAE runs under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, which covers Muslim UAE citizens and Muslim expats unless one spouse insists on their own national law (Article 1). A husband divorces by talaq, which counts as one divorce however often it is repeated (Article 57) and which he must document at court within 15 days (Article 58); a wife can leave by khula' for a consideration, which the court orders if he stubbornly refuses (Articles 65 and 66), or ask the court to end the marriage on a ground in Articles 69 to 80, such as a defect, harm, unpaid dowry, failure to maintain, desertion, absence or imprisonment, or addiction. Before a case reaches the judge, the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance, but that referral is discretionary (Article 8). For the full statutory picture, see our <a href="/en/new-uae-personal-status-law-2025/">new UAE personal status law 2025</a> explainer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="page-layout">
    <main class="main-content">

      <!-- Key facts box -->
      <div class="answer-box">
        <h2>Six things to know about Sharia divorce in UAE from 2025</h2>
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li><strong>In force since 15 April 2025, including pending cases.</strong> Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 replaced Law 28/2005 and also applies to divorce proceedings that had no final judgment when it took effect (Article 1).</li>
          <li><strong>A talaq must be documented within 15 days.</strong> If the husband does not document it at court in time, without an excuse the court accepts, the wife can claim compensation equal to maintenance from the divorce until it is documented (Article 58). Documentation is a duty, not a condition for the divorce to count.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeated talaq counts as one divorce.</strong> Repeated pronouncements, or a divorce coupled with a number, whether spoken, written or signalled, count as a single divorce (Article 57).</li>
          <li><strong>Khula' can be ordered over a refusal.</strong> If the husband stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula' for an appropriate consideration (Article 66); it takes effect as a minor irrevocable divorce (Article 65).</li>
          <li><strong>Family Guidance referral is at the judge's discretion</strong> (Article 8), with no fixed statutory period, and arbitrators in contested harm cases have up to 60 days from appointment to report (Article 72; the old law allowed 90).</li>
          <li><strong>Custody runs to age 18,</strong> and from 15 the child may choose which parent to live with unless the child's interest requires otherwise (Articles 122 and 123).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: Which Law -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The 2024 Law: What Changed and Who It Covers</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> is the Personal Status Law that governs Muslim family matters in the UAE. It came into force on 15 April 2025, replacing <strong>Federal Law No. 28 of 2005</strong>, which had governed Muslim family matters for two decades. It applies to events after that date, and Article 1 also applies it to divorce deeds, divorce proceedings and lineage proceedings that had no final judgment when it took effect, so a divorce case filed earlier but still undecided moves to the new rules.
        </p>
        <p>
          Who does it cover? Article 1 applies it to UAE citizens where both spouses or one of them is Muslim, and to non-UAE citizens unless one spouse insists on applying their own national law or another law permitted in the State. In practice, a British, Egyptian or Pakistani Muslim couple divorcing in a UAE court is under this law unless one of them asks for their home law. Non-Muslim foreigners have the parallel civil track under <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022</a>. For how the two systems fit together, see our <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law overview</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Five rules in the 2024 law that shape a divorce</h3>
        <div class="change-list">
          <div class="change-card">
            <div class="change-num">1</div>
            <div class="change-body">
              <p class="change-title">Khula' when the husband refuses</p>
              <p>Khula' is separation at the wife's request with the husband accepting a consideration, and it takes effect as a minor irrevocable divorce (Article 65). The consideration can be any property; if it is the dowry, giving back what she received is enough and the rest is dropped (Article 66). If the husband stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula' for an appropriate consideration. The children's maintenance and custody cannot be bargained away as part of it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="change-card">
            <div class="change-num">2</div>
            <div class="change-body">
              <p class="change-title">Talaq: one divorce, documented within 15 days</p>
              <p>Repeated pronouncements, or a divorce coupled with a number, count as a single divorce, whether spoken, written or signalled (Article 57). The husband must document the divorce before the competent court within 15 days of it happening (Article 58). If he does not, without an excuse the court accepts, the wife is entitled to compensation equal to maintenance from the divorce until it is documented, and she keeps the right to file her own case to prove the divorce.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="change-card">
            <div class="change-num">3</div>
            <div class="change-body">
              <p class="change-title">Custody now runs to age 18</p>
              <p>Custody now continues to age 18 (Article 123), replacing the old split of roughly 11 for boys and 13 for girls under the 2005 law. From 15 the child may choose which parent to live with unless the child's interest requires otherwise (Article 122). See our <a href="/en/child-custody-uae/">child custody UAE guide</a> for the detail.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="change-card">
            <div class="change-num">4</div>
            <div class="change-body">
              <p class="change-title">Family Guidance referral discretionary, harm arbitration capped at 60 days, addiction listed as a ground</p>
              <p>Referral to Family Guidance is at the supervising judge's discretion under Article 8, where the 2005 law made it mandatory for most claims; the law sets no fixed period for Family Guidance. Separately, in contested harm cases, the two court-appointed arbitrators have up to 60 days from their appointment to report (Article 72; the old law allowed 90), which helps those cases reach a judgment sooner. Addiction is a listed ground for divorce (Article 80), alongside the harm grounds discussed in our <a href="/en/divorce-for-harm-uae/">divorce for harm UAE guide</a>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="change-card">
            <div class="change-num">5</div>
            <div class="change-body">
              <p class="change-title">Maintenance rules spelled out</p>
              <p>Maintenance covers food, clothing, housing, medical treatment and education according to custom (Article 95), and is set by the payer's means, the recipient's condition and the economic situation of the time and place (Article 96). A wife's claim for past maintenance is not heard for more than two years before filing (Article 99), a child's for more than one year (Article 111), and an amount cannot normally be changed within a year of a final ruling (Article 97). See our <a href="/en/alimony-uae/">alimony UAE guide</a> for how maintenance is assessed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Four Types -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Four Types of Sharia Divorce Recognised in UAE</h2>
        <p>
          UAE courts recognise four distinct forms of Islamic divorce. Each has different triggers, different consent requirements, and different financial consequences. Understanding which type applies to your situation determines your strategy. For a plain comparison across the full menu, see our <a href="/en/types-of-divorce-uae/">types of divorce UAE guide</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="divorce-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Who initiates</th>
                <th>Husband consent</th>
                <th>Mahr outcome</th>
                <th>Key notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {divorceTypes.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class={`td-type td-type--${row.flag}`}>{row.type}</td>
                  <td class="td-sm">{row.who}</td>
                  <td class="td-sm">{row.consent}</td>
                  <td class="td-sm">{row.mahr}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Talaq in detail</h3>
        <p>
          Talaq is the husband's divorce. Under Article 58 he must document it before the competent court within 15 days of it happening, and repeated pronouncements, whether spoken, written or signalled, count as a single divorce (Article 57). Documentation does not create the talaq; it records and dates it. If the husband delays without an acceptable excuse, the wife can claim compensation equal to maintenance from the divorce date until it is documented, and she can file her own case to prove the divorce. Article 56 lists situations in which a divorce does not take effect at all, such as a pronouncement under duress, in anger so intense that the husband could not control his words, or while intoxicated.
        </p>
        <p>
          A first or second talaq after intercourse or valid seclusion is revocable: during the iddah the husband can take his wife back by action or by explicit spoken or written words, without a new contract (Articles 60 to 62). If he documented the divorce, he must also document the take-back within 15 days and tell her at once (Article 64). A talaq before intercourse or seclusion is a minor irrevocable divorce, so remarrying needs a new contract and a new dowry (Articles 59 and 60). A third talaq is a major irrevocable divorce: she cannot remarry him until she has completed the waiting period from another husband who had a valid, consummated marriage with her (Article 59). For full details, see our dedicated <a href="/en/talaq-uae/">talaq UAE guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Khula' in detail</h3>
        <p>
          Khula' is the wife's route out of the marriage. Article 65 defines it as separation at the wife's request with the husband's acceptance of a consideration, and it takes effect as a minor irrevocable divorce, so a later reunion needs a new contract and dowry. Under Article 66 the consideration can be any property, but it cannot include giving up the children's rights, maintenance or custody. If the husband stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula' for an appropriate consideration. The spouses document the khula' before the court within 15 days (Article 68). See our full <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula' divorce UAE guide</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dowry rule in Article 66 matters in practice: where the consideration is the dowry, returning what she actually received is enough, and her claim to any unpaid remainder is dropped. After khula' there is no iddah maintenance (Article 101), so the money picture differs from a talaq. For detailed guidance on mahr, see our <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">mahr divorce UAE guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Court divorce: the grounds in Articles 69 to 80</h3>
        <p>
          This is the route for a spouse whose partner will not agree to a talaq or khula'. The 2024 law sets out the grounds article by article:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A serious harmful defect or disease, or one that prevents marital relations such as impotence (Article 69): incurable cases are revoked without a grace period, curable ones can be postponed for up to one year</li>
          <li>Harm (Article 71), with two arbitrators appointed where the harm is disputed, who have up to 60 days to report (Article 72)</li>
          <li>Revocation at the wife's request before intercourse or seclusion, if she returns the dowry she received (Article 75)</li>
          <li>Non-payment of a dowry that is due (Article 76)</li>
          <li>Failure to maintain (Article 77)</li>
          <li>Desertion (Article 78)</li>
          <li>Absence, disappearance or imprisonment of the husband (Article 79)</li>
          <li>Addiction (Article 80)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Each article has its own conditions, and the court applies them to your evidence: police and medical reports, witnesses, and bank records showing missed maintenance are typical. A wife in a consummated marriage whose divorce or revocation was due to the husband can also claim compensation (mut'a) of up to one year's maintenance (Article 102). For full guidance on the harm route, see our <a href="/en/divorce-for-harm-uae/">divorce for harm UAE guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Family Guidance Section -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Family Guidance Section: When the Judge Refers You</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Family Guidance Section</strong> (Dar al-Tawjeh al-Asari) is a counselling and settlement service attached to the courts. Under Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, before a case goes to the judge, the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance if a settlement seems feasible. It is not compulsory, and the law sets no fixed period for it. Under the 2005 law referral was mandatory for most claims. Urgent and temporary matters, such as temporary maintenance or custody orders, and cases where reconciliation is not foreseeable, such as cases to prove a marriage or prove a divorce, are not referred. For the wider process, see our <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">divorce in UAE guide</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the spouses settle, the agreement is recorded in a report signed by them and the counsellor and approved by the supervising judge; it then has the force of an enforceable writ and cannot be appealed unless it contradicts the law (Article 8). If they do not settle, the case goes on to the court. The 60-day limit you may read about belongs to a later stage: in a contested harm claim the court appoints two arbitrators, and Article 72 gives them up to 60 days from their appointment to report (the old law allowed 90).
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>What happens at Family Guidance sessions</h3>
          <p>A family counsellor meets the spouses to go through the rights and duties of each and to look for an amicable settlement. You are not in court, and the counsellor does not decide anything. In Abu Dhabi, the Judicial Department says a family case is registered through its e-services, sessions can be held by video call, and the requests are referred to the competent court, at the request of either spouse, when no settlement is reached.</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          If your spouse will not take part, no settlement can be reached and the matter moves on to the court; ask the Family Guidance office or your lawyer how that is recorded in your emirate. Our <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">Family Guidance Section guide</a> covers the practical steps.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Financial Rights -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Financial Rights in Sharia Divorce: What the Wife is Owed</h2>
        <p>
          Islamic law gives the wife defined financial rights on divorce. They are legal obligations that the husband owes and that UAE courts enforce through the execution judge if necessary. Our <a href="/en/wife-rights-after-divorce-uae/">wife rights after divorce UAE guide</a> sets these out in full.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Financial right</th>
                <th>When it falls due</th>
                <th>Key rules</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {financialRights.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-bold">{row.right}</td>
                  <td class="td-sm">{row.timing}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.notes}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Mahr: when it falls due</h3>
        <p>
          Mahr (the dowry) is the property the husband pays the wife under the marriage contract, and it is hers (Article 45). The spouses can agree to defer all or part of it; if they agreed no deferral and set no time, it is due on request (Article 46). Whatever was agreed, Article 46 says the dowry becomes due on irrevocable separation or the death of a spouse. A revocable talaq does not end the marriage until the iddah expires (Article 59), so the deferred dowry falls due when the iddah ends without a take-back, or at once on a third talaq or a khula'. The full dowry is confirmed by intercourse, valid seclusion or death, and a wife divorced before intercourse gets half the specified dowry (Article 47).
        </p>
        <p>
          If the husband does not pay a judgment for the dowry, the wife applies to the execution judge, who can use the enforcement measures in the Civil Procedure Code. For enforcement in practice, see our <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">mahr divorce UAE guide</a> and our guide to <a href="/en/unpaid-alimony-enforcement-uae/">unpaid alimony enforcement</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Iddah: what it means and what it costs the husband</h3>
        <p>
          The iddah is the waiting period after separation during which a woman may not remarry (Article 81). After a divorce it applies only if the marriage was consummated or there was valid seclusion (Article 82). For a woman who menstruates it lasts three periods of purity; for one past menopause or who has never menstruated, three lunar months (Article 84); for a pregnant woman it ends with the birth or a miscarriage (Article 85). A widow who is not pregnant observes four months and ten days (Article 86). For a court divorce, the iddah runs from the date the judgment becomes final (Article 83).
        </p>
        <p>
          Iddah maintenance is due after a revocable divorce. After an irrevocable divorce it is due only if she is pregnant, until the birth, and there is none after khula' or death (Article 101). In all cases she may live in the marital home during the iddah, or in a suitable home if the marital home is unsuitable. During the iddah of a revocable talaq the husband can take her back (Article 61); after an irrevocable talaq or khula' he cannot. See our dedicated <a href="/en/iddah-waiting-period-uae/">iddah waiting period UAE guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Custody -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Child Custody in Sharia Divorce</h2>
        <p>
          Islamic law distinguishes between hadhana (physical care and upbringing) and wilaya (legal guardianship). These are separate concepts that the 2024 law preserves.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Hadhana</strong> under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024: after separation custody goes first to the mother, then the father, then the maternal and paternal grandmothers, and the court may depart from that order in the child's interest (Article 114). Custody continues to age 18, and beyond it for a disabled child (Article 123); from 15 the child may choose which parent to live with unless the child's interest requires otherwise (Article 122). A custodian must meet the conditions in Article 113, and custody lapses if a condition is lost or if, for example, the custodian moves somewhere with the intention of residence where the child's interests cannot be met (Article 115).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Wilaya</strong> (guardianship) generally remains with the father, who handles the child's affairs, discipline and education, but a custodian mother has educational guardianship (Article 112), and disputes go to the Judge of Summary Matters by order on petition. Travel is the usual flashpoint: a custodial parent may take the child abroad with the other parent's written approval, and if that is refused the court can authorise travel for periods totalling up to 60 days a year, with a guarantee of return, or longer for the child's benefit, treatment or a necessity (Article 116). The guardian may keep the child's passport except when it is needed for travel (Article 117).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Child maintenance (nafaqat al-awlad)</strong> is the father's obligation regardless of which parent has physical care (Article 106). The court sets it from his means and the child's needs (Article 96), and a claim for a child's past maintenance cannot reach back more than one year before filing (Article 111). See our <a href="/en/child-support-uae/">child support UAE guide</a>, and for relocation and international cases, our <a href="/en/child-custody-uae/">child custody UAE guide</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>If you fear your spouse will take the children abroad</h3>
          <p>If you are in divorce proceedings and believe your spouse may take your children to another country without your consent, speak to a lawyer at once about an urgent application to the court. Under Article 116 a custodial parent needs the other parent's written approval or the court's permission to travel with the child, and urgent and temporary applications are not sent to Family Guidance first (Article 8). Act before serving divorce papers if the risk is imminent. The UAE is not a party to the Hague Child Abduction Convention, so the return of a child taken abroad cannot be pursued through that route. See our <a href="/en/child-travel-consent-uae/">child travel consent guide</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: Recognition -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Recognition of UAE Sharia Divorces Abroad</h2>
        <p>
          Whether another country accepts a UAE Sharia divorce is decided by that country's own law, not by the UAE. Procedures and conditions differ from country to country, so check with that country's consulate in the UAE or a lawyer there before you rely on the divorce at home, and especially before remarrying.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first steps are the same almost everywhere: obtain the court's divorce judgment or certificate, have it certified and attested in the UAE, translate it if needed, then file it with the home-country registry. The UAE is not a party to the Hague Apostille Convention, so there is no UAE apostille: the chain is court or notary certification, attestation by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MOFA), and then legalisation by the relevant embassy or consulate. For attestation steps, see our <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">UAE divorce certificate attestation guide</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For England and Wales, recognition of overseas divorces is governed by Part II of the Family Law Act 1986, and the tests depend on how the divorce was obtained and on the spouses' links to the UAE; our guide on how a <a href="/en/uae-divorce-recognised-uk/">UAE divorce is recognised in the UK</a> explains them. Elsewhere, a lawyer in the country concerned can tell you whether a UAE court divorce needs a separate recognition step.
        </p>
      </section>

